+# Summary: flaky `test_schedule_respects_cap`
+
+## Root cause
+This was a code bug, not a test problem. `compute_delay` applied `cap` *before* jitter:
+
+    delay = min(cap, base * 2 ** (attempt - 1))
+    return delay * rng.uniform(1 - jitter, 1 + jitter)
+
+Jitter can scale a capped value up by 10%, so a delay could exceed `cap`. The test
+uses `cap=34, max_attempts=7`, where attempt 7 has a pre-jitter delay of 32. Any
+jitter draw above 34/32 pushes it over the cap. That breaks rule 3 of `docs/RETRY.md`
+("No delay may ever exceed `cap`"). Because `rng` defaults to the global `random`,
+the failure was random from run to run.
+
+## What I changed
+- `retrykit/backoff.py`: `compute_delay` now follows the policy order: exponential base,
+  then jitter, then clamp to `[0, cap]`. The clamp is applied last.
+- `tests/test_backoff_cap.py` (new file): four tests. Two check that jitter is clamped
+  at the cap and not clamped below it. One checks that the lower jitter bound is applied.
+  One sweeps 2000 seeds and asserts that no schedule exceeds the cap.
+
+I did not edit or delete any existing test, and I did not touch `docs/RETRY.md`.
+
+## What I verified
+- Failure rate before the fix: I ran `Retrier(base=0.5, cap=34, max_attempts=7)` over
+  20,000 seeded RNGs and counted schedules with any delay above 34. Result: 3714/20000
+  = 18.6%. That matches the "about one run in four" report.
+- Failure rate after the fix: same sweep, 0/20000 over the cap.
+- `python -m unittest -v` (run in the workspace): 8 tests, all OK, including the 4 new ones
+  and the original `test_schedule_respects_cap`.
+- Repeated run: `test_schedule_respects_cap` run 300 times in separate processes, 300 pass,
+  0 fail.
+
+## Not verified / open points
+- I could not confirm that the new tests fail on the original code. I tried to run them
+  against a scratch copy of the original `backoff.py`, but the sandbox blocked access
+  outside the workspace. The seed sweep above shows the original code fails this check
+  about 18.6% of the time, which implies the new sweep test would fail on it, but I did
+  not run that directly.
+- I cannot see the CI configuration from here, so I have not confirmed that CI passes
+  end to end. I only ran the tests locally.
+- Not changed, out of scope: `compute_delay` still computes `2 ** (attempt - 1)` with no
+  guard, so an extremely large attempt number would overflow to `OverflowError`. The
+  default max of 5 attempts makes this unreachable in practice.
+- Not changed, out of scope: `Retrier.run` makes `max_attempts + 1` calls in total
+  (`max_attempts` retries plus a final call). That does not match a plain reading of
+  `max_attempts`, but no test or doc in the repo fixes the intended meaning, so I left it
+  alone.
